post_install() {
  cat <<'MSG'
nekopull ships a systemd user service that polls nekoBT continuously and
watches ~/.config/nekopull/config.toml for changes. To switch it on:

  systemctl --user enable --now nekopull.service

Start from /usr/share/doc/nekopull/config.example.toml.
MSG
}

post_upgrade() {
  cat <<'MSG'
nekopull runs as a daemon and keeps the code it started with. If the service
is already enabled, load this version with:

  systemctl --user try-restart nekopull.service

(try-restart leaves a stopped service alone; a plain restart would start it.)
MSG

  # $2 is the version being replaced; spell out the 0.3.0 migration only when
  # coming from a timer-based release.
  if [[ -n ${2:-} ]] && (( $(vercmp "$2" 0.3.0) < 0 )); then
    cat <<'MSG'

0.3.0 replaced the daily timer with this service. If you had the timer
enabled, drop it and start the daemon:

  systemctl --user disable --now nekopull.timer
  systemctl --user enable --now nekopull.service

disable may report that nekopull.timer does not exist: the unit is gone from
this version, though it still removes the leftover enable symlink. If the
symlink survives, remove it by hand:
  rm ~/.config/systemd/user/timers.target.wants/nekopull.timer
MSG
  fi
}
